WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:17.290 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hey everyone its Peter Brown reading  Alessandro barbosa's review of the  forest the broad premise of the forest  is far from unique the plane crash lands  on a seemingly deserted island and you a  lone survivor have to figure out a way  to survive it's not long however until  this familiar setup turns an unexpected  corner and the forest evolves into a  uniquely harrowing adventure that you  won't soon forget  blood-curdling screeches fill the night  and glowing eyes watch you from a  distance your new home island isn't as  empty as it appears  cannibals inhabit the grassy fields and  pristine lakes around you watching your  every move they're the source of the  forest ever-present tension you might  expect mindless monsters like this to  attack on sight but their behavior is  erratic sometimes they'll charge forward  to unsettle you during daylight but stop  just outside striking distance to simply  stare at you in silence other times they  might faint a retreat before leaping up  into surrounding trees and getting  behind you the settlement you eventually  create is invaded by waves of larger  more imposing foes who you pray will  retreat as you thin their numbers the  forest enemies aren't easy to predict  which makes each encounter a thrill  the breath of enemy types is impressive  too and the forest isn't straight from  leaning into the weird either as you  explore the island more and dive into  terrifying pitch black caverns enemies  transform into terrifying body horror  figures amalgamations of appendages that  bellow deep disturbing howls they're  frightening to behold and even scarier  to fight the force does a good job of  trickling out these surprises while  you're already struggling to manage your  vital meters and resources it's also  imperative that you keep a close eye on  the quality of the resources you find  not every berry bush contains a bounty  that won't poison you and that all still  water is safe to drink meat you gather  from hunted animals will rot if not  cooked quickly none of the resulting  illnesses are serious enough to dissuade  you from eating questionable food to  stave off death but needing to think  twice about what you eat has an  additional layer to the standard  hunter-gatherer gameplay base  construction is equally engrossing due  to the sheer number of structures you're  able to build the forest doesn't Gate  it's creative building with elusive  blueprints instead from the outset it  gives you a notebook filled with  outlines for structures knowing how to  build shelters gardens and defenses  isn't the challenge the forest wants to  impose instead it shifts this on to the  risky task of acquiring resources to  build at all chopping down trees for  logs or scouting a route to clean water  is paramount in your first few days on  the island and once you establish  yourself this goal shifts to fortifying  your position with complex spike traps  and tree swings the construction of  which is deeply satisfying building has  a tangible effect on the island in  several ways resources like small game  and shrubs will respawn over time but  larger trees will remain felled for the  entirety of your stay you might turn a  dense forest into an open field of  stumps not long after you start but  that'll also give enemies a clear line  of sight into your doings the more you  impose yourself in the island the more  aggressive your aggressors become  patrols will grow and the more monstrous  creatures will emerge from their caves  for an all-out assault the force doesn't  force you to play in any specific way  though so a more reserved nomadic  approach is sometimes safer and more  viable  crafting smaller items plays a big part  when it comes to personal safety to your  inventory screen allows you to combine  items you've collected to create new  tools from something as simple as  combining a few sticks and stones to  make an axe to creating high-powered  explosives using a combination of  wristwatches electrical bores and spare  change the number of items you can both  collect and craft is vast but the  inventory page eventually becomes  cumbersome and overwhelming to navigate  and with only four customisable hotkeys  you don't actually have a lot of easy  access to what you want in a pinch at  the very least the forest never feels  too overbearing you'll always be able to  depend on your crafted weapons as they  aren't hampered by durability your  pocket letter will help you see in the  dark never running out of vital fluid  either this frees you from the burden of  worrying about the lifespan of any  potential upgrades you can make you're  incentivized to craft upgrades to  weapons that improve their damage and  speed while other combinations that you  create more illuminating torches with  limited burning times exploration and  survival games is usually tied only to  your immediate well-being but the forest  features a narrative slowly uncovered by  exploration and incidental environmental  storytelling abandoned camps are great  hunting ground for modern resources but  they also hint at past events cannibal  villages feature gory displays of  victims but also contain vital clues  small photographs videotapes and  magazines that flesh out a conspiracy  with the island at the center with a kid  there the forest doesn't have to be a  lonely experience and it offers  cooperative player for up to eight  people the time spent getting a  fortified settlement up and running is  drastically reduced then but remains  just as compelling cooperative play does  however deflate tension larger groups of  enemies become easier to deal with and  the fear of diving into caves alone is  undercut by both voice chat and the fact  that enemies don't really scale  according to your party size the forest  might be silly fun with friends but it's  best when playing alone yeah guys I got  this  [Music]  guys take a break I got this super swell  now guys  there's a lot to think about when it  comes to surviving in the forest but the  balance between each of its interlocking  parts keeps the game moving at a  riveting pace for every danger the  island offers there's a smart solution  around a corner combined with  unpredictable enemy actions and  captivating horror set pieces the forest  strikes a compelling balance between  survival and horror  [Music]

